                }
              },
              "startIndex": 125349
            },
            {
              "endIndex": 125424,
              "paragraph": {
                "elements": [
                  {
                    "endIndex": 125424,
                    "startIndex": 125423,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "\n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "paragraphStyle": {
                  "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT",
                  "lineSpacing": 100,
                  "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"
                }
              },
              "startIndex": 125423
            },
            {
              "endIndex": 126122,
              "paragraph": {
                "elements": [
                  {
                    "endIndex": 126122,
                    "startIndex": 125424,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "^ My experience is that coding AIs are slow, expensive and produce WET code. Their context needs to be 100x before they'll truly be great. They continually forget to stick to your rules. They cannot prioritise a vast memory of all the things all the time, so they make mistakes and write crappy code. They don't think globally, they don't think like a great engineer. They just print syntax, locally, and they suffer terrible cognitive tunneling. They're not creative enough to think of great ways to solve their own problems or approach things. They can continually improve their own code, which betrays that they cannot think and prioritize all the necessary things needed to write amazing code.\n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "paragraphStyle": {
                  "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT",
                  "lineSpacing": 100,
                  "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"
                }
              },
