            {
              "endIndex": 234348,
              "paragraph": {
                "elements": [
                  {
                    "endIndex": 234348,
                    "startIndex": 234326,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "The 15 Hour Work Week\n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "paragraphStyle": {
                  "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT",
                  "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"
                }
              },
              "startIndex": 234326
            },
            {
              "endIndex": 234350,
              "paragraph": {
                "elements": [
                  {
                    "endIndex": 234350,
                    "startIndex": 234348,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": " \n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "paragraphStyle": {
                  "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT",
                  "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"
                }
              },
              "startIndex": 234348
            },
            {
              "endIndex": 235279,
              "paragraph": {
                "elements": [
                  {
                    "endIndex": 234359,
                    "startIndex": 234350,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "In 1930, ",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  },
                  {
                    "endIndex": 234569,
                    "startIndex": 234359,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "John Maynard Keynes wrote “Economic Possibilities for our Grandchildren,” predicting that productivity growth would be so powerful that by the early twenty-first century the workweek would fall to fifteen hours",
                      "textStyle": {
                        "backgroundColor": {
                          "color": {
                            "rgbColor": {
                              "blue": 0.9529412,
                              "green": 0.8862745,
                              "red": 0.8117647
                            }
                          }
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  },
                  {
                    "endIndex": 234844,
                    "startIndex": 234569,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": ". He was directionally correct about productivity growth, but profoundly wrong about labor market implications. Rather than working dramatically less, societies consumed dramatically more. Why? Because rising productivity lowered costs and expanded the consumption frontier. ",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  },
                  {
                    "endIndex": 234955,
                    "startIndex": 234844,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "Preferences shifted toward higher quality goods, new services, and previously unimaginable forms of expenditure",
                      "textStyle": {
                        "backgroundColor": {
                          "color": {
                            "rgbColor": {
                              "blue": 0.827451,
                              "green": 0.91764706,
                              "red": 0.8509804
                            }
                          }
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  },
                  {
                    "endIndex": 235279,
                    "startIndex": 234955,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": ". Leisure increased modestly, but material aspiration expanded far more. History suggests productivity gains do not automatically translate into labor withdrawal or demand collapse as they alter the composition of demand, expand real incomes and generate new industries. Keynes underestimated the elasticity of human wants.\n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "paragraphStyle": {
                  "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT",
                  "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"
                }
              },
              "startIndex": 234350
            },
            {
              "endIndex": 235280,
              "paragraph": {
                "elements": [
                  {
                    "endIndex": 235280,
                    "startIndex": 235279,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "\n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "paragraphStyle": {
                  "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT",
                  "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"
                }
              },
              "startIndex": 235279
            },
            {
              "endIndex": 235291,
              "paragraph": {
                "elements": [
                  {
                    "endIndex": 235291,
                    "startIndex": 235280,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "Conclusion\n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "paragraphStyle": {
                  "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT",
                  "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"
                }
              },
              "startIndex": 235280
            },
            {
              "endIndex": 235293,
              "paragraph": {
                "elements": [
                  {
                    "endIndex": 235293,
                    "startIndex": 235291,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": " \n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "paragraphStyle": {
                  "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT",
                  "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"
                }
              },
              "startIndex": 235291
            },
            {
              "endIndex": 236151,
              "paragraph": {
                "elements": [
                  {
                    "endIndex": 236151,
                    "startIndex": 235293,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "For AI to produce a sustained negative demand shock, the economy must see a material acceleration in adoption, experience near-total labor substitution, no fiscal response, negligible investment absorption, and unconstrained scaling of compute. It is also worth recalling that over the past century, successive waves of technological change have not produced runaway exponential growth, nor have they rendered labor obsolete. Instead, they have been just sufficient to keep long-term trend growth in advanced economies near 2%. Today’s secular forces of ageing populations, climate change and deglobalization exert downward pressure on potential growth and productivity, perhaps AI is just enough to offset these headwinds. The macroeconomy remains governed by substitution elasticities, institutional response, and the persistent elasticity of human wants.\n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
                "paragraphStyle": {
                  "direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT",
                  "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"
                }
              },
              "startIndex": 235293
            },
