                  {
                    "endIndex": 10587,
                    "startIndex": 9744,
                    "textRun": {
                      "content": "Venezuela, holder of the world's largest proven oil reserves (over 300 billion barrels) and abundant other natural resources, has seen its once-thriving oil industry collapse under Nicolás Maduro's socialist regime. Nationalization and mismanagement drove away foreign investment, causing active drilling rigs to plummet from dozens in the mid-2010s to as low as zero (and recently just a handful), while production fell from over 3 million barrels per day to around 1 million. This deliberate shift toward state-controlled socialism effectively halted meaningful extraction and trade of its vast resources, starving the economy and depriving the global market of critical supplies at a time when the world urgently needs more energy—rendering the situation increasingly untenable until Maduro's recent capture by U.S. forces in January 2026.\n",
                      "textStyle": {}
                    }
                  }
                ],
